1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,419
Book Three, Proposition One.

2
00:00:02,419 --> 00:00:05,061
To find the center of a given circle.

3
00:00:05,061 --> 00:00:11,130
Let A B C be the given circle; it is required to find the center of the circle A B C.

4
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:18,148
Draw a chord A B through the circle at random, and bisect it at D by One Ten.

5
00:00:18,148 --> 00:00:25,260
From D, draw D C at right angles to A B, draw it through to E, and bisect C E at F.

6
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:27,574
I say that F is the center.

7
00:00:27,574 --> 00:00:35,200
For suppose it is not, but, if possible, let G be the center, and join G A, G D, and G B.

8
00:00:35,620 --> 00:00:50,500
Because A D equals D B, D G is common, and the radii G A and G B are equal, by One Eight the adjacent angles A D G and G D B are equal, so angle G D B is a right angle by Definition Ten.

9
00:00:50,500 --> 00:01:01,700
Yet angle F D B is also a right angle by construction, making angle F D B equal to angle G D B, the greater to the less—which is impossible.

10
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:06,340
Thus G is not the center, nor is any other point except F.

11
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:10,020
Therefore F is the center of the circle A B C.

12
00:01:10,020 --> 00:01:11,700
Which was to be done.

13
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:21,320
Proposition Three One opens Euclid's geometry of the circle, along with its celebrated Porism: the perpendicular bisector of any chord passes through the center.

14
00:01:21,320 --> 00:01:29,834
As Simson observed, because only the definition of a circle is available at the outset of Book Three, the proof must proceed by reductio ad absurdum.

15
00:01:29,834 --> 00:01:43,720
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G ninety-eight unites segment bisection and perpendicularity with Side-Side-Side congruence, becoming the constructive anchor for forty-four downstream theorems across Books Three, Four, Six, Twelve, and Thirteen.

16
00:01:44,140 --> 00:01:57,607
